---
// src/pages/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide/index.astro — REBUILT from the approved board
// record (data/boards/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide.json, approved 2026-09-20).
// Hand-written and NOT regenerated by `npm run extract`: the slug is listed in
// data/facts/rebuilt.json, so `migration_parity.py` skips it and `facts_preserved_check.py`,
// `link_parity_check.py` and `verbatim_set_check.py` are its gates instead.
//
// THIS IS THE BREED GUIDE, AND IT OWNS THE BREED-FACTS CELLS THE REST OF THE SITE CITES.
// `sections[breed-facts].table.rows` is the source /blue-staffy-health-uk/ reads for its own
// lifespan tile and its hero aside — it cuts "12 to 14 years" out of this record's Lifespan
// cell rather than retyping it — so those row labels and that cell wording are a CONTRACT, not
// a layout choice. The three counter tiles on this page are cut from the same table, by the
// same kind of derivation, so the page that OWNS the figures and the page that CITES one of
// them can never print two different readings of one row.
//
// THE STRATEGY IS "THE HONEST OWNER'S MANUAL" (`brief.strategy`). Of the four breed pages
// scanned, two ignore UK law entirely, none makes the American Bully distinction in any depth,
// none names a bad fit plainly, and only one has an FAQ worth the name. The migrated body
// already carried all four of those, buried under four badges — ZERO BANNED, #1 AFFECTION,
// 100% TESTED, 12+ YEARS — with no evidence attached to any of them.
//
// WHAT THIS PAGE MAY NOT SAY, and none of it is an oversight (`brief.out_of_scope`):
// no £850 to £1,200 banner and no £850 to £2,500+ purchase band (both below or beside the
// locked prices, Known Issue 11, `dropped.prices`); NONE of the migrated cost-of-ownership
// table — puppy classes, food, insurance, routine vet care, parasite control, daycare,
// boarding and the monthly total built from them — because no file on disk holds one of those
// figures (rule 9); no hand-typed guarantee length (data/settings.json `guarantee_label`
// is the only source, and this page does not state it); no named
// insurance provider; no litter age and no scarcity line; no former city in any sentence
// (Known Issue 16 — it survives only inside one image FILENAME that working rule 11 requires
// to be reused unchanged); no prevalence figure, percentage or prognosis for any condition;
// no temperament "statistic" and no bite or aggression figure in either direction; no
// newsletter block; and no enquiry form — the contact page carries the one form the form audit
// measures, and `talk-to-us` links it.
//
// HEALTH-TEST NAMES ARE THE TWO AND ONLY THE TWO. L-2-HGA and HC-HSF4, and the detail behind
// them is /blue-staffy-health-uk/'s, which this page LINKS rather than restates. A breed guide
// reproducing the health page's table would be two pages claiming one table.
//
// WORKING RULES 11 AND 14. All TEN recorded photographs are carried at their original public
// paths with their original alt text, and the page's ONE embed is carried at its original id —
// `g9iV9RVr_Sk`, mounted through `VideoEmbed` in the record's own `video-breed-guide` section,
// directly below the opening where the migrated body had it. `facts_preserved_check.py` reports
// by name any id a rebuilt page drops.
//
// WORKING RULE 15 (faithful rewrite). The VERBATIM SET is carried WORD FOR WORD and is READ
// OFF THE RECORD (`sections[].heading`, `sections[].verbatim_opening`, the tree nodes' own
// pair, `assets[].alt`, and the `data/faq.json` rows the record names) rather than retyped
// here, so a curly quote cannot drift between the record the breeder approved and the page that
// ships. Nine deviations are recorded in `verbatim.changed` with their reasons. THE H1 IS NOT
// ONE OF THEM: `h1.pick` is variant 0, which IS the migrated H1 word for word, so no `h1` row
// is owed, none is written, and the slug's keyword phrase is already inside it ("The Ultimate
// UK Staffordshire Bull Terrier Guide Handbook").
//
// WORKING RULE 16. The hero is `H-GD3` — the text-led PANEL with the photo in a narrow column
// on the left and an aside beside the copy, in a card — and the counter is `C-GD3`, figure
// cards inside a card. Both are the interior-guide family's own arrangements, resolved through
// `pickedStyle()`. Since the three guides were re-boarded (Known Issues 30 and 35) no other
// page takes H-GD3: the health guide is on H-GD1 and the buying guide on H-GD2.
//
// THE ASIDE IS THE RECORD'S OWN `hero.aside`, AND NOT ONE ITEM OF IT STATES A FIGURE. It is a
// three-line contents list — the arrangement's own name is "text-led card with a contents
// aside" — so under amendment 10.6 every item is a plain string and none carries or needs a
// `source`. That is the difference from the health page's aside, whose three rows are facts and
// are each derived and asserted at build; the check below is that this one has not quietly
// become that one. The three approved CHIPS are rendered in the same box as a single line,
// because `Hero` shows a chip row only for `ledge: 'chips'`: on H-GD3 the prop is handed over
// and dropped, and a claim the breeder approved is not worth losing to an arrangement. The FULL
// contents list is `PageNav`, three lines below the hero, which `PageShell` mounts from
// `sectionsFromRecord(record)` — sixteen rows in a 15rem column is taller than the whole band
// before anything else is put in it.
//
// THREE FIGURES, ALL CUT FROM THIS PAGE'S OWN TABLE AT BUILD (amendment 10.5, and the lesson
// da9c34e wrote down). The record's `n` is a hand-written number, true on the day it was
// boarded; each tile is derived from the cell its `source` names, and a reading that no longer
// matches what the breeder approved throws with both readings in the message.
//
// EIGHT SECTIONS CARRY NO PICK. `history`, `temperament`, `daily-care`, `health`,
// `legal-status`, `bully-comparison`, `choosing-breeder` and `talk-to-us` have
// `options.pick: null`, so no arrangement was ever chosen for them and none is invented: they
// render with no `bl-box` class at all, exactly as the health page's nine no-pick sections do.
//
// SCHEMA IS Article + FAQPage. `brief.schema.types` is WebPage + Article + FAQPage; BaseLayout
// already emits the `#webpage` node with the git-derived dates, so the two minted here are the
// other two. THE ARTICLE CARRIES NO `dateModified`, deliberately: BaseLayout drops its own
// WebPage node the moment a page's schema mentions one (`selfDated`), and an Article whose
// `isPartOf` then pointed at a node nobody emitted is the dangling `@id` `schema_check.py`
// blocks on. Dates stay in the one place git writes them. No VideoObject: the record does not
// ask for one, and an uploadDate lifted from the old theme's markup is not a fact this repo
// keeps — the correction 1c500e5 made to the about page's video caption.
//
// H4-H6 ARE WRITTEN HERE, not in the record (amendment 2.1): the approved outline stops at the
// H3 the breeder read. Every ladder sentence traces to the migrated body or to a file under
// data/, and the H6 lines all open on the record's own `tuple.h6_prefixes` entry, "Breed Note:".
// Every heading renders through `titleCase()` (amendment 4b).
import PageShell from '../../layouts/PageShell.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/kit/Hero.astro';
import CounterStrip from '../../components/kit/CounterStrip.astro';
import DataTable from '../../components/kit/DataTable.astro';
import InfoCard from '../../components/kit/InfoCard.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../../components/kit/VideoEmbed.astro';
import PuppyCard from '../../components/kit/PuppyCard.astro';
import Testimonial from '../../components/kit/Testimonial.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/kit/Faq.astro';
import { sectionsFromRecord } from '../../lib/sections';
import { pickedStyle, approvalInForce } from '../../lib/pickedStyle';
import { bakedAssets, bakedSrcset, inPublic, BODY_SIZES } from '../../lib/assets';
import { recordText, countedFigures, sourceKey, ledgeText, breedFactsCell,
  sentenceAnchor as S, type LedgeEntry } from '../../lib/recordText';
import { boxClass } from '../../lib/boardStyles';
import { titleCase as T } from '../../lib/headings';
import { loadFaq } from '../../lib/faq';
import { SITE, gbp, abs, type PuppyRow } from '../../lib/site';
import pageDates from '../../../data/page-dates.json';
import { videoObject } from '../../lib/video';
import puppiesJson from '../../../data/puppies.json';
import reviewsJson from '../../../data/reviews.json';
import settings from '../../../data/settings.json';
import prices from '../../../data/price-matrix.json';
import record from '../../../data/boards/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide.json';

const path = '/uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide/';
// The picked meta variants and H1, BY INDEX from the approval IN FORCE — never retyped here.
// `approvalInForce()`, not `record.approval`: the record went back to the board for its hero
// and its video (Known Issues 35 and 38) with its approval carried under `approval_previous`,
// and what the page is called was never the question (spec §9 amendment 11a).
const approved = approvalInForce(record);
const title = record.meta_set.titles[approved.meta!.title];
const description = record.meta_set.descriptions[approved.meta!.description];
const h1 = record.h1.variants[approved.h1];

// The ten migrated photographs, by slot, at their ORIGINAL public paths with the alt text the
// record baked (working rule 11; all ten alts are elements of the verbatim set). The
// legal-status alt is the one the record repaired: the migrated wording ended on "clearly
// states:" and then stopped, so a screen reader was read a colon where the sign's words should
// have been. A missing slot throws at build.
const asset = bakedAssets(record);
const litter = asset('guide-litter');
const handbook = asset('guide-handbook');
const heritage = asset('guide-history');
const breederCare = asset('guide-breeder-care');
const healthCertified = asset('guide-health-certified');
const legalStatus = asset('guide-legal-status');
const mythsPhoto = asset('guide-myths');
const bullyComparison = asset('guide-bully-comparison');
const kcPapers = asset('guide-kc-papers');
const costInfographic = asset('guide-cost-infographic');

// The carried wording, read off the record rather than retyped (see the rule 15 note above).
const { sec, heading, opening, node, nodeOpening } = recordText(record);

// THE REVIEW, BY NAME rather than by index. The record's intent states MODE: single on
// data/reviews.json entry 1 — The Victoria Family, Manchester — because it is the only one of
// the three that speaks to temperament and early socialisation, which is what the temperament
// and daily-care sections above it claim. WHICH review is the record's sentence inside
// `record_hash`; HOW the block is drawn is the pick.
interface Review { quote: string; name: string; place: string; source: string }
const victoria = (reviewsJson as Review[]).filter((r) => r.name === 'The Victoria Family');
if (victoria.length !== 1) {
  throw new Error('uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide: data/reviews.json no longer holds the '
    + 'The Victoria Family row the record names');
}

// THE NINE FAQ ROWS THE RECORD NAMES, BY ID, in the record's order. Eight carry the migrated
// accordion's own questions word for word (rule 15); `guide-banned-breed` is the outline's, and
// the migrated page never asked it. The ninth migrated question — the most common cause of
// death — is a `verbatim.changed` drop, because "cancer, old age-related issues, and heart
// problems" is a mortality claim about a breed with no source on disk. The answers are data.
const faqSection = record.sections.find((s) => s.id === 'questions')!;
const faqRows = loadFaq();
const faqs = faqSection.tree.map((n) => {
  const row = faqRows.find((r) => r.id === n.heading);
  if (!row) throw new Error(`uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide: data/faq.json has no row ${n.heading}`);
  return row;
});

// ── THE THREE FIGURES, CUT FROM THE CELLS THIS PAGE OWNS ────────────────────────────────────
// Each counter tile's `source` names a cell of this record's own breed-facts table, so the
// figure is READ OUT of the cell rather than copied off the record — and because
// /blue-staffy-health-uk/ cuts its lifespan out of the same cell by the same rule, the day that
// wording moves BOTH pages stop building rather than quietly disagreeing about one row.
const factsTable = (sec('breed-facts') as unknown as
  { table: { caption: string; columns: string[]; rows: string[][] } }).table;
const statRows = (sec('stats') as unknown as
  { stats: { n: string; label: string; source: string }[] }).stats;
const CELL_RE = /#cell\(sections\[breed-facts\]\.table\.rows, ([A-Za-z]+)\)$/;
/** One reading of one row, shared with /blue-staffy-health-uk/, which cites the Lifespan cell.
 *  `breedFactsCell` matches the label at the START of the row label, so `Height` finds "Height
 *  at the shoulder" and a label that has stopped existing throws instead of resolving to a
 *  neighbouring row. */
const fact = (label: string) => breedFactsCell(record, label, 'uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide');
/** The unit the APPROVED TILE prints, taken off the record's own `n`. NOTHING in this file names
 *  a unit: a `{Height: 'in'}` map here would be the unit typed in two places, so re-cutting the
 *  table from inches to centimetres would have left the tile still reading "in" and every gate
 *  satisfied. `range()` checks the abbreviation against the cell's own unit and throws if the
 *  table has moved under it. */
const TILE_UNIT = /^\d+\u2013\d+\s+(.+)$/;
const COUNTED: Record<string, string> = Object.fromEntries(statRows.map((row) => {
  const m = CELL_RE.exec(String(row.source));
  if (!m) {
    throw new Error(`uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide: the counter row "${row.label}" cites `
      + `${row.source}, which is not one of this page's own breed-facts cells`);
  }
  const unit = TILE_UNIT.exec(row.n);
  if (!unit) {
    throw new Error(`uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide: the counter row "${row.label}" prints `
      + `"${row.n}", which is not a "<lo>-<hi> <unit>" range and has no unit to check the cell's `
      + 'own against');
  }
  return [sourceKey(row.source), fact(m[1]).range(unit[1])];
}));
const counted = countedFigures(COUNTED, 'uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide');
const counterStats = statRows.map(counted);
// The height, weight and lifespan cells WHOLE, for the three places the page states them in a
// sentence rather than as a tile. Same cells, same single source, so the prose and the counter
// cannot drift apart either.
const heightCell = fact('Height').text;
const weightCell = fact('Weight').text;
const lifespanCell = fact('Lifespan').text;

// ── THE HERO'S OWN DATA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const heroSection = sec('top') as unknown as
  { hero: { chips: LedgeEntry[]; aside: { title: string; items: LedgeEntry[] } } };
const heroChips = heroSection.hero.chips.map(ledgeText);
const heroAside = heroSection.hero.aside;
// EVERY ITEM IS A PLAIN STRING, AND THAT IS CHECKED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. An aside item stating
// a figure would owe a `source` (amendment 10.6); this aside is the contents list and states
// none, so a `{text, source}` item appearing in it would mean the record had started making a
// claim here that the page is not deriving — which is the thing that rule exists to stop.
const asideItems = heroAside.items.map((item) => {
  if (typeof item !== 'string') {
    throw new Error(`uk-staffordshire-bull-terrier-guide: the hero aside item "${ledgeText(item)}" `
      + 'carries a source — this aside is the contents list and states no figure, so an item that '
      + 'has become a claim needs deriving here rather than printing');
  }
  return item;
});

// The candidate list is built from the files that exist, not spelled into the markup (the fix
// 1c500e5 made): a hand-written `srcset` is a filename duplicated away from its file, and a 404
// candidate is invisible to `img-srcset-within-2x` because the master is what paints.
// The panel hero paints its photo in a 224px column above 900px and full width below it, which
// is the `sizes` Hero writes for this arrangement (the fix 8c71fcd made). The 440 candidate
// already baked beside the 612px master covers both readings inside 2x, so nothing is
// re-encoded and nothing is renamed.
const heroSrcset = bakedSrcset(litter, [440], inPublic);
// NO BODY `srcset`. Every in-body master here is between 436px and 640px wide against a column
// that paints at about 420px, so the widest ratio on the page is 1.52x — inside
// `img-srcset-within-2x` on the master alone. Offering a candidate that is not on disk is the
// 404 `bakedSrcset` refuses to write.

const sections = sectionsFromRecord(record);
/** The git-derived dates BaseLayout reads for its WebPage node, for the Article to agree with. */
const articleDates = (pageDates.routes as Record<string, { datePublished: string }>)[path];

// Each section's approved arrangement, resolved through the same `boxClass()` the board preview
// called: what the breeder signed off is what ships. Only the eight sections that carry a pick
// are ever asked for one.
const style = (id: string) => pickedStyle(record, id);
const box = (id: string) => boxClass(style(id));
const heroStyle = style('top');
const counterStyle = style('stats');
const videoStyle = style('video-breed-guide');
const reviewStyle = style('owner-review');

const video = (sec('video-breed-guide') as unknown as
  { video: { id: string; title: string; caption: string } }).video;
const available = (puppiesJson as PuppyRow[]).filter((p) => p.status === 'Available');
const town = SITE.location_label.split(' · ')[0];
const hours = SITE.hours.replace('Mo-Su ', 'from ').replace('-', ' to ');
/** Thousands separator, as `PuppyCard` writes a price: 1500 is a number, £1,500 is money. */
const delivery = `£${gbp(settings.delivery_min_gbp)} to £${gbp(settings.delivery_max_gbp)}`;

// Article + FAQPage + VideoObject. The FAQPage is built from the SAME nine rows the accordion
// renders; data/faq.json keeps the sentence-case wording, which is what `name` reads
// (amendment 6g). The VideoObject is built from the record's own `video` block through
// `src/lib/video.ts`, so the schema and the video sitemap describe one id in one spelling.
//
// `@id`s ARE ABSOLUTE, and the two REFERENCES are not, deliberately. Every node this site
// mints of its own — `#business`, `#website` — is absolute, and these three now match. The
// `isPartOf` and `mainEntityOfPage` pointers have to match the spelling of the node they point
// AT, and BaseLayout writes its WebPage `@id` relative (`/slug/#webpage`); a reference in the
// other spelling is the dangling `@id` `schema_check.py` blocks on. When BaseLayout's node goes
// absolute these follow it, and the gate says so the moment they do not.
const WEBPAGE = { '@id': `${path}#webpage` };
const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#article`,
    headline: h1,
    description,
    inLanguage: 'en-GB',
    // `datePublished` ONLY. It is git-derived, from the same map BaseLayout reads, so the
    // Article and the WebPage cannot state two different first-published dates. There is no
    // `dateModified` here on purpose: BaseLayout drops its own WebPage node the moment a
    // page's schema mentions one (`selfDated`), and the Article's `isPartOf` would then point
    // at a node nobody emitted.
    ...(articleDates ? { datePublished: articleDates.datePublished } : {}),
    isPartOf: WEBPAGE,
    mainEntityOfPage: WEBPAGE,
    author: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#business` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#business` },
  },
  videoObject(video, abs(path)),
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    '@id': `${abs(path)}#faq`,
    mainEntity: faqs.map((r) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: r.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: r.a },
    })),
  },
];

---
<PageShell
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={path}
  ogType="article"
  schema={pageSchema}
  sections={sections}
>
  {/* 01 top — hero, picked style H-GD3 (a text-led panel in a card: the photograph in a narrow
      column on the left, the copy beside it, the contents aside in a box of its own). The aside
      is a SLOT (amendment 8) and what goes in it is the record's own `hero.aside`. No CTA
      buttons and no trust-chip row: the record gives the hero neither, and the arrangement's
      ledge is `aside` (amendment 6a). The recorded refresh delta is the LAYOUT — a magazine
      opening where the health page splits its hero in two. */}
  <section slot="hero" id="top" data-section-label="The breed guide" class={box('top')}>
    <Hero
      as="h1"
      eyebrow={`A UK breeder's breed guide · ${SITE.location_label}`}
      title={T(h1)}
      {/* ONE SENTENCE. The desktop lede is `-webkit-line-clamp: 2` and a clamp HIDES its
          overflow, so a third line disappears rather than pushing anything past the ceiling —
          invisible on the page, and only a measurement finds it. */}
      lede="What a Staffordshire Bull Terrier is to live with, written by the people who raise them."
      image={litter.file}
      imageAlt={litter.alt}
      imageWidth={litter.w}
      imageHeight={litter.h}
      imageSrcset={heroSrcset}
      layout={heroStyle.layout.hero ?? 'panel'}
      align={heroStyle.layout.align ?? 'left'}
      media={heroStyle.layout.media ?? 'left'}
      ledge={heroStyle.layout.ledge ?? 'aside'}
    >
      {/* THE ASIDE IS THE PAGE'S MARKUP, and it carries the record's chips as well as its items.
          `Hero` renders the chip row only for `ledge: 'chips'` — correctly, because a hero
          showing two ledges is neither arrangement — so on H-GD3 the prop is handed over and
          dropped. The three chips are rendered here as a single line rather than a wrapped row,
          because a row costs the card two lines it does not have. */}
      <div slot="aside">
        <p class="aside-title">{heroAside.title}</p>
        <ul class="aside-items">
          {asideItems.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="aside-chips">{heroChips.join(' · ')}</p>
      </div>
    </Hero>
    <h2>{T(heading('top'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('top')}</p>
    <p>
      We breed this one dog, in {town}. Our litters are KC registered; every puppy is
      microchipped and vet checked before it goes. What follows is the build, the temperament,
      the work a day asks for, the conditions the breed is screened for, the law, and five
      things worth asking any breeder, us included. Where a figure is not on a file we
      keep, it is not here.
    </p>
  </section>

  {/* 02 video-breed-guide — video, picked style S3 (the click-to-play facade: a thumbnail and a
      play button, the player injected on the first press). It was S2, the player full width on
      a steel band, until the user's ruling R8 (Known Issue 38): S2 loaded the player with the
      page and cost Lighthouse Best Practices 4 points, and S3 is what working rule 14 makes the
      default. `VideoEmbed` takes `play` from the pick, so the markup did not change.
      Working rule 14: the id is `g9iV9RVr_Sk`, the one id data/facts records for this page,
      carried unchanged and in the place the migrated body had it, directly below the opening and
      ahead of the breed table. The recorded refresh delta is the motif. */}
  <section id="video-breed-guide" data-section-label="The breed itself, on film" class={box('video-breed-guide')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('video-breed-guide'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <VideoEmbed
        id={video.id}
        title={video.title}
        caption={video.caption}
        play={videoStyle.layout.play ?? 'facade'}
      />
      <p>
        It is a short film of our puppies rather than a spoken guide: the breed itself, before
        the writing below sets out what it is and what it asks of you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 03 takeaways — takeaways, picked style S1 (stacked statement cards). FOUR cards, which is
      what the record's tree carries, each the one-line form of a section below it. The H2 and its
      opening are the migrated page's own: "Why Choose a Staffordshire Bull Terrier?" is an
      element of the verbatim set. Two of the four cards state their figures by reading the
      breed-facts cells above rather than by repeating them. The recorded refresh delta is the
      layout. */}
  <section id="takeaways" data-section-label="Why choose a Staffy" class={box('takeaways')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('takeaways'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">{opening('takeaways')}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <div class="bl-cards">
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 0).heading)}
          body={`${heightCell} at the shoulder, ${weightCell}. A dog you can lift, and a dog that can pull you over.`}
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 1).heading)}
          body="Affectionate, loyal and wired for company. It is the trait the breed is loved for, and the one that decides whether your house suits it."
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 2).heading)}
          body="Not a banned breed under the Dangerous Dogs Act 1991, and legal to own, breed and import here."
        />
        <InfoCard
          kind="fact"
          heading={T(node('takeaways', 3).heading)}
          body={`Our own facts table puts a well-bred, well-kept Staffy at ${lifespanCell}.`}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 04 stats — the counter strip, picked style C-GD3 (figure cards inside a card, each label
      under its figure): the interior-guide family's arrangement the health page did NOT take,
      which is what keeps working rule 16's promise between two pages sharing a hero id. All
      three figures are cut at build from this page's own breed-facts cells and checked against
      the number the breeder approved. The recorded refresh delta is the accent. */}
  <section id="stats" data-section-label="The breed in three figures" class={box('stats')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('stats'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">Three readings off the table further down, for anyone who came for those.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <CounterStrip
        stats={counterStats}
        tiles={counterStyle.layout.tiles ?? 'card'}
        label={counterStyle.layout.label ?? 'under'}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 05 breed-facts — table, picked style S2 (zebra rows inside a card). THE TABLE THIS SITE
      CITES: the health page reads its Lifespan cell, and the three tiles above read Height,
      Weight and Lifespan, so the row labels and the cell wording are a contract rather than
      copy. Nine rows, every figure the migrated body's own nine numbered traits, nothing added.
      It stacks into labelled rows below 640px — `DataTable` carries `.stack-table`
      unconditionally (working rule 13). The recorded refresh delta is the container. */}
  <section id="breed-facts" data-section-label="Breed information at a glance" class={box('breed-facts')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('breed-facts'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <p>{opening('breed-facts')}</p>
      <DataTable
        caption={factsTable.caption}
        columns={factsTable.columns}
        rows={factsTable.rows}
      />
      <img
        class="bl-img"
        src={handbook.file}
        alt={handbook.alt}
        width={handbook.w}
        height={handbook.h}
        sizes={BODY_SIZES}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <p>
        <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/breed-standards/terrier/staffordshire-bull-terrier/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the registry's own breed standard for the Staffordshire Bull Terrier")}</a> is
        what those rows describe, in the standard's own words.
        <a href="#talk-to-us">Ask us about the breed</a> if a dog you have met does not match
        them.
      </p>

      <h3>{T(node('breed-facts', 0).heading)}</h3>
      <p>
        Blue is one of the colours in the row above and nothing more: not a separate breed, a
        separate standard or a separate size. A blue Staffy is measured against these nine rows,
        as a brindle one is.
      </p>

      <h4>{T('The Table Is The Dog, Not The Advert')}</h4>
      <p>
        Every row is checkable against an animal standing in front of you, which is why a guide
        opens on a table rather than on adjectives.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('A Dog Well Outside These Rows Is Worth A Question')}</h5>
      <p>
        Not a disqualification and not an accusation — but the comparison further down this page
        is the thing to read before any money changes hands.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Breed Note: Height is taken at the withers')}</h6>
      <p>That is where the shoulder blades meet, not the top of the head, which flatters by inches.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 06 history — standard, and NO PICK: `options.pick` is null on this record, so no
      arrangement was ever chosen for it and none is invented here. The H2 and the opening are
      migrated wording. The one date on the page is the migrated body's own — formal recognition
      by the registry in 1935 — and no other is written, because no file we keep holds one. The
      recorded refresh delta is the motif: image-top, reading as a chapter opener. */}
  <section id="history" data-section-label="History and heritage">
    <h2>{T(heading('history'))}</h2>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={heritage.file}
      alt={heritage.alt}
      width={heritage.w}
      height={heritage.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <p>{opening('history')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('history', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The breed was put together in the industrial Midlands — Staffordshire and the Black Country
      — by people who wanted a bulldog's grip and a terrier's quickness in one animal small
      enough to keep in a back yard. That is the whole of the design brief, and the dog on your
      sofa is still built to it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('history', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Here is the part that explains the modern temperament, and there is nothing sentimental in
      it. A dog used in those sports was handled constantly — lifted, washed, separated, carried
      home — by the people who kept it, and one that turned on those people was not bred from. So
      tolerance of human handling was selected for as hard as anything else was, generation after
      generation, and it outlived the cruelty that produced it. The affection is not a modern
      softening of the breed. It is the oldest thing in it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('history', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The breed gained formal recognition by the registry in 1935, which is the point at which it
      stopped being a type and became a breed with a written standard behind it.
      <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/search/breeds-a-to-z/breeds/terrier/staffordshire-bull-terrier/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the registry's own Staffordshire Bull Terrier breed page")}</a> is
      where that standard and what a registration covers are set out.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('One Date In This Section, Because One Is What We Have')}</h4>
    <p>
      1935 is the only year the history carries. The century is the one this guide has always
      given, and the rest of the story is told without dates, because a year invented to tidy a paragraph is
      exactly what a reader cannot check.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('The Reputation Came From The Job, Not From The Dog')}</h5>
    <p>
      What the breed was used for is a fact about people. Saying it plainly is what leaves the
      myths section able to answer anything; skipping it is what leaves them standing.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: The old name gives the recipe away')}</h6>
    <p>"Bull and terrier" says what was crossed with what, long before anybody wrote a standard.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 07 temperament — standard, NO PICK. The five traits the migrated body names, then the home
      that fits them, then the one home that does not. The H2 and its opening are migrated
      wording, as are the "Ideal UK Home Setup" H3 and its own opening. NO TEMPERAMENT STATISTIC
      and no bite claim anywhere: the migrated body gives no figure and none is invented. The
      recorded refresh delta is the density. */}
  <section id="temperament" data-section-label="Temperament and the right home">
    <h2>{T(heading('temperament'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('temperament')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={breederCare.file}
      alt={breederCare.alt}
      width={breederCare.w}
      height={breederCare.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The bond is the breed. A Staffy attaches itself to a household rather than to a room in
      it, and the dog that follows you from the kitchen to the bathroom is behaving as designed.
      Everything else here follows from that one trait.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      For a dog built like this, the ambition is remarkably small: the sofa, a person, ideally a
      blanket. Visitors are greeted as arrivals rather than as intruders.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Brave, and alert enough to tell you somebody is at the door. A guard dog, no — the breed
      is far too pleased to see whoever it turns out to be. If you want a dog that keeps
      strangers off the path, this is the wrong one.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Fetch, tug and outright silliness carry on well past the first birthday and, in most of
      the dogs we have bred, well past the third.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('temperament', 4)}</p>
    <p>
      In practice that means a garden fenced high enough and solidly enough to hold a determined,
      muscular dog, or a walking routine reliable enough to stand in for one; people in and out
      of the house through the day; and floors you are relaxed about. No square footage and no
      minimum garden size is given here, because no file we keep holds either.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-uk-breeders/">Read about the two parents our puppies are socialised around</a> to
      see the house ours are raised in.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 5).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      First-time owners can certainly succeed with this breed, given a real commitment to two
      things kept up rather than merely started: early socialisation, and reward-based training.
      <a href="https://www.rspca.org.uk/adviceandwelfare/pets/dogs/puppy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the RSPCA's independent advice on socialising a puppy")}</a> is
      written by people with nothing to sell you, which is why we point at them rather than at
      ourselves. Flat living works on the same terms, given the exercise and the mental
      stimulation below.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 6).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Long hours alone. That is the honest answer, and it is the only hard one on this page. A
      breed selected for wanting people does not learn to stop wanting them: a Staffy left alone
      all day gets bored, then anxious, then destructive, usually in that order. If the
      house is empty from morning to evening most days, this is the wrong breed.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('temperament', 7).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Famously good with children, and supervised around them as any dog should be. Other
      animals want slow, early introductions: the prey drive is real, and a nice temperament
      does not argue you out of it.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('Who The Breed Is Wrong For Belongs In The Guide')}</h4>
    <p>
      A page that will not name a bad fit is a sales page in a guide's clothes, and of the four
      breed pages we read only one said plainly who should not buy one.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Company Is The Requirement, Not The Garden')}</h5>
    <p>
      The flat with somebody in it beats the house with nobody in it, every time, and it is the
      inversion most buyers do not expect.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: Tell us your week, not your square footage')}</h6>
    <p>The hours a house stands empty are what we ask about, because they are what the dog notices.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 08 daily-care — standard, NO PICK. The four ordinary jobs — exercise, feeding, training and
      grooming — plus the migrated page's own life-stage H3 and its opening. NO BRAND, NO GRAM
      FIGURE, NO MEAL COUNT AND NO WORMING INTERVAL: the migrated body's life-stage schedule is
      `dropped.text`, a veterinary timetable with no source on disk, and the health page is where
      a puppy's own position is stated. The one figure here is the migrated body's own — at least
      an hour of vigorous exercise a day, split into two. The recorded refresh delta is the
      layout. */}
  <section id="daily-care" data-section-label="Daily care">
    <h2>{T(heading('daily-care'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('daily-care')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      At least one hour of vigorous exercise a day, ideally split into two sessions. Vigorous is
      the word doing the work there: a slow amble round the same block twice is an hour on paper
      and nothing at all in the dog. Brisk walking somewhere that smells different each time,
      running in a secure space, and play with a person in it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The other half of the job is mental. Agility, obedience, flyball and scent work all suit a
      breed that is quick, biddable and easily bored, and ten minutes of thinking settles this
      dog more reliably than another mile does. Puzzle toys at home and recall practice off-lead
      in a safe, enclosed area do the same work in smaller pieces. It is also the half most
      often skipped.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Food formulated for the life stage, in measured meals at set times rather than a bowl left
      down, and treats used sparingly, as training rewards rather than snacks. This breed will eat whatever is
      in front of it and puts weight on readily. No brand and no quantity is named here: no file
      we keep holds one, the bag states a guide and your vet knows your dog.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-blog-guides/">Our blog guides go further on feeding and training a new puppy</a> for
      the first fortnight in detail.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Positive reinforcement, started early, and identical from every adult in the house. Staffies
      are intelligent and eager to please, and they are also strong-willed, which together mean
      they learn what you are actually teaching rather than what you meant to teach. Basic
      commands and lead manners are the foundation; consistency is what makes them stick.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A short, close-lying coat with no undercoat wants a weekly brush and very little else. Baths
      when the dog has found something worth a bath, and not on a schedule. Nails, ears and teeth
      are the parts owners put off, and the teeth are the ones that cost the most later.
      <a href="https://www.pdsa.org.uk/pet-help-and-advice/looking-after-your-pet/puppies-dogs/medium-dogs/staffordshire-bull-terrier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the PDSA's veterinary guide to caring for a Staffordshire Bull Terrier")}</a> covers
      the same ground with a vet behind it.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('daily-care', 5).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('daily-care', 5)}</p>
    <p>
      A puppy does far less than the adult hour and builds up to it, because the joints are still
      arriving; an older dog does less again and wants it in smaller pieces. No meal counts,
      worming intervals or visit frequencies are printed here — those belong to your own vet, who
      is looking at your dog rather than at a breed.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('The Hour Is A Floor, Not A Target')}</h4>
    <p>
      It is what the guide has said since the old site, and it is the figure worth planning a week
      around. A dog that gets more than it is not being spoilt.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Weight Is The Thing Most Entirely In Your Hands')}</h5>
    <p>
      Ribs easy to feel and not to see is the whole of the test. It takes ten seconds and it is
      worth doing weekly rather than annually.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: The hour is better in two halves')}</h6>
    <p>Morning and evening, rather than one march: a dog worked twice is calmer for longer either side.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 09 health — standard, NO PICK. A SHORT VERSION OF THE HEALTH PAGE AND NOT A SECOND COPY OF
      IT: the two DNA tests by name, both our parents DNA tested, the skin and the joints, the
      vaccination course, and then the link. NO PREVALENCE FIGURE, NO PERCENTAGE AND NO PROGNOSIS
      — the migrated body gives none. NO THIRD TEST: the breed has screens for two, and
      /blue-staffy-health-uk/'s two-row table is the contract the whole site reads. The H2 and
      the opening are migrated wording. The recorded refresh delta is the layout. */}
  <section id="health" data-section-label="Health and ethical breeding">
    <h2>{T(heading('health'))}</h2>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={healthCertified.file}
      alt={healthCertified.alt}
      width={healthCertified.w}
      height={healthCertified.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <p>{opening('health')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('health', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/health-and-dog-care/health-dog-care/health/getting-started-with-health-testing-and-screening/dna-testing/dna-test-l-2hga/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the registry's own page on the L-2-HGA DNA test")}</a> names
      the first: L-2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria. For hereditary cataracts,
      <a href="https://www.royalkennelclub.com/health-and-dog-care/health-dog-care/health/getting-started-with-health-testing-and-screening/dna-testing/dna-test-hc-hsf4/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the registry's own page on the HC-HSF4 DNA test</a> does
      the same job. Two is the number of DNA screens this breed has, and a third named on a
      breeder's site is a credential nobody can check.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Both our parents are DNA tested for both conditions, which is the one claim in this
      section about our own dogs rather than about the breed.
      <a href="/blue-staffy-health-uk/">Our health page sets out every test we run and what each looks for</a>,
      names each test against a dog rather than against the kennel, and is where what the two
      tests mean for a puppy is explained at length.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Skin allergies and demodicosis are the breed's other common complaints. A coat this short
      shows irritation early, which is a gift if somebody is looking at it, and the first thing
      worth raising with a vet is usually what is in the bowl.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Beyond the two DNA tests, the health page records two BVA/KC screens for our own dogs: an
      eye examination for inherited eye disease the HC-HSF4 test does not cover, and patella
      grading for knee health. Neither prints a score, there or here, because a score is a number
      and no file we keep holds one. No hip or elbow score is claimed anywhere on this site.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('health', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The course protects against distemper, parvovirus, leptospirosis and hepatitis. Its first
      part is done before a puppy leaves us and the rest belongs to your own vet. Every puppy is
      also microchipped before it goes, which is legally required in the UK and often done by
      the breeder, and the number is ready to transfer into your name.
      <a href="https://www.pdsa.org.uk/pet-help-and-advice/pet-health-hub/other-veterinary-advice/dog-vaccines" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the PDSA's guide to dog vaccinations")}</a> sets
      the whole schedule out, written by a charity with no puppies to sell.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('A Guide Names The Conditions And Then Stops')}</h4>
    <p>
      No percentage, prevalence or prognosis appears beside a condition anywhere on this page. This
      guide has never given one, we hold none, and a number invented to sound authoritative is worse
      than the silence it replaces.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('Two Tested Parents Is A Fact, Not A Policy')}</h5>
    <p>
      A DNA test is taken by a named dog, which is why ours are listed against the two parents
      rather than as a house rule.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: Ask for both certificates by name')}</h6>
    <p>Each names a dog, a test and a result, and all three should match what you were told.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 10 legal-status — standard, NO PICK. The legal position first, then the practical
      consequence, then the four myths. The H2 and its opening are migrated wording, as are the
      "Legal Status in the UK" H3 and its own paragraph — which names the Act and the four banned
      types — and the myths H3 and its opening. The four myths are answered in a sentence each and
      NOT ONE is answered with a statistic: no file on disk holds one, and a bite figure quoted in
      the breed's defence is the same unevidenced claim pointing the other way. The renting advice
      is the one checkable sentence of the migrated block; the pet-CV and pet-deposit tips and the
      "DEFRA-compliant breeders like us" clause are `dropped.text`. The recorded refresh delta is
      the container. */}
  <section id="legal-status" data-section-label="Staffies and UK law">
    <h2>{T(heading('legal-status'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('legal-status')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={legalStatus.file}
      alt={legalStatus.alt}
      width={legalStatus.w}
      height={legalStatus.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('legal-status', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('legal-status', 0)}</p>
    <p>
      <a href="https://www.gov.uk/control-dog-public/banned-dogs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{S("the government's own list of banned dog types")}</a> is
      where to read that rather than take our word for it, and it is the list the paragraph above
      is describing.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('legal-status', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Legal to own is not the same as allowed where you live. A landlord, a housing association
      or a block's own rules can restrict the breed even though the law does not, and a verbal
      yes at a viewing is worth nothing six months later. Ask for the pet policy in writing
      before you sign anything, and ask for it while you are still choosing between places
      rather than after a deposit has gone in — a policy you have read is a question answered,
      and a policy nobody will put in writing is an answer too. That single sentence is the
      whole of the housing advice this page gives, because it is the only part of it we can
      stand behind: we are breeders, the tenancy is yours, and the pet clauses in it are
      between you and whoever wrote them.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('legal-status', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('legal-status', 2)}</p>
    <p>
      Four of them, each answered in a sentence, and each answered without a statistic. They are
      worth answering rather than ignoring because they are not harmless: a myth is what a
      landlord half-remembers, what a neighbour repeats across a fence, and what a family
      quietly decides on before they have met one of these dogs. The honest position is that
      the first is about how a dog is bred and raised, the second is about anatomy that does
      not exist, the third is a reason for closer attention rather than less, and the fourth is
      contradicted by every training class this breed turns up to.
    </p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={mythsPhoto.file}
      alt={mythsPhoto.alt}
      width={mythsPhoto.w}
      height={mythsPhoto.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h4>{T(node('legal-status', 2).children[0].heading)}</h4>
    <p>
      Behaviour is decided by breeding, socialisation, training and handling, not by a breed label
      — which is why everything above this line is about those four things and none of it is about
      the label.
    </p>

    <h4>{T(node('legal-status', 2).children[1].heading)}</h4>
    <p>
      No dog has a locking jaw. The mechanism does not exist in any breed's anatomy, so there is
      nothing here to be careful about: the claim is simply false.
    </p>

    <h4>{T(node('legal-status', 2).children[2].heading)}</h4>
    <p>
      They feel it, and they are stoic about it, which is a reason to watch this breed more closely
      rather than less — a Staffy will carry on wagging through something another dog would tell
      you about.
    </p>

    <h4>{T(node('legal-status', 2).children[3].heading)}</h4>
    <p>
      Intelligent, eager to please and quick to learn, given reward-based training. The
      strong-willed part is real and is a reason for consistency, not a ceiling on what the dog can
      be taught.
    </p>

    <h5>{T('Four Myths, And No Statistics')}</h5>
    <p>
      None of the four is answered with a figure, deliberately. We hold no dataset on dog bites or
      on breed behaviour, and borrowing one to win an argument would be the same fabrication in a
      friendlier direction.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: The best answer is your own dog in public')}</h6>
    <p>A well-socialised, well-trained Staffy out in public challenges more outdated perceptions in a year, and educates more people, than any paragraph here will.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 11 bully-comparison — standard, NO PICK. The confusion that has legal consequences in the
      UK. Both H3 names are migrated wording and elements of the verbatim set. The Staffy side's
      build figures are read out of this page's own table; the American Bully side states origin,
      recognition, variability and the ban, and states NO measurement, because no file on disk
      holds one for a type with no standard behind it. NO EFFECTIVE DATE for the XL Bully ban: the
      migrated body gave one as a range, and a date is exactly the thing this page does not invent.
      The recorded refresh delta is the density. */}
  <section id="bully-comparison" data-section-label="Staffy and American Bully">
    <h2>{T(heading('bully-comparison'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('bully-comparison')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={bullyComparison.file}
      alt={bullyComparison.alt}
      width={bullyComparison.w}
      height={bullyComparison.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('bully-comparison', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A UK breed, made in nineteenth-century England and written into a standard by the registry. {heightCell} at the shoulder, {weightCell}. Legal to own, breed and import here.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('bully-comparison', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A US type, developed in the 1990s out of American Pit Bull Terriers, American Staffordshire
      Terriers and other bulldog types. Not recognised by the registry as a breed, and recognised
      instead by clubs of its own. Because no single standard sits behind it, size and build vary
      widely, which is why no measurement for it appears here.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('bully-comparison', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Because the law treats the two differently. Many American Bully types, the XL in
      particular, are banned in the UK under the Dangerous Dogs Act 1991. A banned dog is
      identified by type rather than by paperwork, so what a dog is called matters far less than
      what it is.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('bully-comparison', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      An advert offering an oversized Staffy, an XL Staffy or a Staffy cross. The breed does not
      come in an oversized version, and the table higher up this page is the whole of the range.
      Walk away, and go and look at dogs whose parents and papers you can see.
      <a href="/buy-blue-staffy-puppies-uk/">See the registered Staffordshire Bull Terrier puppies we have here</a> if
      you would rather compare against real dogs than against a description.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('The Difference Is Legal Before It Is Cosmetic')}</h4>
    <p>
      Getting this wrong is not an embarrassment about pedigree, it is a dog seized. That is why a
      breed guide carries the comparison at all, and not one of the four pages we read does.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('The Ancestry Is Shared, Which Is Why The Confusion Exists')}</h5>
    <p>
      Both trace back to bull and terrier crosses, so the family resemblance is real. A shared
      great-grandparent is not a shared legal status.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: Ask to see both parents')}</h6>
    <p>Two dogs in front of you settle a question that a photograph and a word never will.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 12 choosing-breeder — standard, NO PICK. The five tests the migrated body names, written so
      a reader can apply them to ANYBODY, us included, and saying which of the five we can
      evidence and where. The H2 and the opening are migrated wording. `dropped.links` strikes the
      shared chat transcript the migrated body offered as its source for what registration covers;
      what it does and does not cover is stated here in our own words instead. The recorded refresh
      delta is the accent. */}
  <section id="choosing-breeder" data-section-label="Choosing an ethical breeder">
    <h2>{T(heading('choosing-breeder'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('choosing-breeder')}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={kcPapers.file}
      alt={kcPapers.alt}
      width={kcPapers.w}
      height={kcPapers.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h3>{T(node('choosing-breeder', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Both parents, both DNA tests, named against each dog rather than a sentence on a
      website. A breeder who says the parents are clear and cannot produce the paper has told you
      nothing at all. Our two tests are on the health page, named against the dogs they belong to.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('choosing-breeder', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      The original registration papers, with the names on them matching the dogs in front of you.
      A KC-registered litter has a record of parentage behind it, and that is all registration
      is: it certifies who the parents were, tests nothing and grades nothing. A breeder quoting
      registration and health screening in one breath is answering a question you did not ask.
      <a href="https://www.thekennelclub.org.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Kennel Club, the registry our litters are registered with</a> holds
      the register those papers come from.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('choosing-breeder', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      In a house, underfoot, with the washing machine and the doorbell and people going past. A
      puppy raised in an outbuilding meets the world for the first time in the back of your car.
      Ask where the litter sleeps, and ask to see it there.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('choosing-breeder', 3).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Advice for the life of the dog, and a puppy taken back if you ever cannot keep it. That
      second half is the one buyers forget to ask about and the one that says the most.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('choosing-breeder', 4).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      A breeder who does not interview you is not choosing where the puppy goes, which means the
      only test being applied is whether your money clears.
      <a href="/">Our own answers to those five are on the home page</a>, and the fifth is the one
      we would rather be held to than any of the others.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('Five Tests You Can Use On Us')}</h4>
    <p>
      An earlier version of this section ended by naming us as the answer. It is worth more
      as five questions a reader takes to every breeder they meet, this one included.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('A Refusal Is An Answer')}</h5>
    <p>
      Nobody is obliged to show you anything. What somebody declines to show, once you have asked
      politely, is the most useful thing you will learn that afternoon.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: Bring the five with you')}</h6>
    <p>Written down and in order, because a checklist is hard to recall with a puppy in your lap.</p>
  </section>

  {/* 13 owner-review — reviews, picked style S2 as the BED and as the MODE (the grid on a steel
      band, `mode: 'grid'`, which is one of the axes `RENDERED_AXES.reviews` lists): the pick
      decides how the block is drawn and it is read off the StyleDef rather than hard-coded, which
      is the correction 645a988 made. The section's `options.note` argues for S1 and the PICK is
      S2; the pick is the answer the breeder gave and the note is left for a re-approval with
      something else to carry. WHICH review is the record's own sentence inside `record_hash` —
      entry 1, The Victoria Family of Manchester, the only one of the three that speaks to
      temperament and early socialisation. The other two slots are not opened, so no
      REVIEW_PLACEHOLDER is counted here. */}
  <section id="owner-review" data-section-label="One family on the temperament" class={box('owner-review')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <p class="bl-eyebrow">The temperament section, from the other side</p>
      <h2>{T(heading('owner-review'))}</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <Testimonial mode={reviewStyle.layout.mode ?? 'grid'} reviews={victoria} />
      <p>
        Everything above about how this breed behaves is a breeder describing their own dogs. One
        family in Manchester, named, describing theirs, is a different kind of sentence.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 14 puppies — puppies, picked style S1 (the three-up card grid), filtered on `status` from
      data/puppies.json rather than hard-coding a count, which is what the record's note asks for.
      The cards' own links are grid data and are exempt from link parity (spec §9 amendment 6b).
      NO per-puppy Offer markup — the listing page owns it. The recorded refresh delta is the
      layout. */}
  <section id="puppies" data-section-label="The Staffies we are raising now" class={box('puppies')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('puppies'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">A guide to a breed ends better on actual dogs.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      <div class="bl-cards">
        {available.map((p) => <PuppyCard slug={p.slug} />)}
      </div>
      <p>
        <a href="/blue-staffy-pup-sale-uk/">what a puppy costs and what is included</a> sets the
        deposit and the delivery bands out in one place.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 15 questions — faq, picked style S3 (the accordion in a card with a jump list). Nine rows of
      data/faq.json in the record's order: eight carry the migrated accordion's own questions word
      for word and one, `guide-banned-breed`, is the outline's. The ninth migrated question — the
      most common cause of death — is a `verbatim.changed` drop. The accordion's answers sit
      outside this section's word band (amendment 4a). The recorded refresh delta is the
      container. */}
  <section id="questions" data-section-label="Frequently asked questions" class={box('questions')}>
    <div class="bl-head">
      <h2>{T(heading('questions'))}</h2>
      <p class="bl-lede">{opening('questions')}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="bl-body">
      {/* The section's own words, which the accordion answers do not supply: `board_gate` counts
          an accordion's answers outside the band (amendment 4a), so a section rendering nothing
          but rows reads as having said nothing. No new claim is made here. */}
      <p>
        Eight of these nine are questions the old guide was asked, kept in the words they were
        put to us, and one — whether the breed is banned — is ours. A tenth has gone: it asked
        what most often kills a Staffy, and we hold no mortality data for a breed. Every answer
        restates something set out above rather than adding to it.
      </p>
      <Faq items={faqs} />

      <h4>{T('Nothing Down Here Is New')}</h4>
      <p>
        Anything worth knowing is above, in a section with a heading on it. Where a claim lives
        only behind a disclosure triangle, somebody has chosen not to say it out loud.
      </p>
      <h5>{T('The Missing Row Is Missing Deliberately')}</h5>
      <p>
        It is the one question on the old list we could not answer from anything we hold, and an
        answer we cannot evidence is worth less than a gap.
      </p>
      <h6>{T('Breed Note: Ask the one that is not here')}</h6>
      <p>No accordion describes your house and your week, and that is usually what the answer turns on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* 16 talk-to-us — standard, NO PICK, and the close. The two locked prices, the refundable
      deposit and the delivery band, read out of data/price-matrix.json and data/settings.json
      rather than typed. NOT ONE of the migrated page's own cost figures is restated anywhere on
      this page: `dropped.prices` strikes the £850–£2,500+ purchase estimate and the whole
      running-cost table, and the migrated "Initial Costs" heading is carried with its parenthesis
      removed (`verbatim.changed`). The H2, the "True Cost" H3 and both their openings are migrated
      wording. NO FORM: the contact page carries the one form the form audit measures, and this
      section links it. The recorded refresh delta is the motif. */}
  <section id="talk-to-us" data-section-label="Begin your Blue Staffy adventure">
    <h2>{T(heading('talk-to-us'))}</h2>
    <p>{opening('talk-to-us')}</p>

    <h3>{T(node('talk-to-us', 0).heading)}</h3>
    <p>{nodeOpening('talk-to-us', 0)}</p>
    <img
      class="bl-img"
      src={costInfographic.file}
      alt={costInfographic.alt}
      width={costInfographic.w}
      height={costInfographic.h}
      sizes={BODY_SIZES}
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />

    <h4>{T(node('talk-to-us', 0).children[0].heading)}</h4>
    <p>
      Roman, Byrd and Ince are £{gbp(prices.male_gbp)}; Vennie, Christa and Cheryl are
      £{gbp(prices.female_gbp)}. A deposit of £{gbp(settings.deposit_gbp)} holds one dog by name and is
      {settings.deposit_refundable ? 'refundable' : 'non-refundable'}. Those are the only purchase
      figures this site prints. The running costs the old version of this page estimated are gone
      from it, because nobody here worked them out and nobody here can check them.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('talk-to-us', 1).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      Collection in {town}, or {settings.delivery_note} at {delivery}.
      <a href="#puppies">See the puppies we are raising now</a> and you will know which dog you
      are asking about before you ask.
    </p>

    <h3>{T(node('talk-to-us', 2).heading)}</h3>
    <p>
      <a href="/uk-blue-staffy-breeders-contact/">Write to us with the question this guide did not answer</a> —
      that is what a guide is for, and the questions people send are what the next version of it
      gets written from. The address is {SITE.email}. {SITE.breeder_name} reads it herself,
      {hours}, between feeds; there is no number on the site because there is not one to give yet.
    </p>

    <h4>{T('A Guide That Names Its Own Price')}</h4>
    <p>
      A breed guide that never says what a puppy costs sends a reader who has just decided straight
      back to a search engine, which serves nobody.
    </p>
    <h5>{T('An Email Is Not A Commitment')}</h5>
    <p>
      Writing joins no queue and holds no dog. A puppy is reserved when a deposit is placed
      against its name, and not a moment sooner.
    </p>
    <h6>{T('Breed Note: Tell us about the week, not just the dog')}</h6>
    <p>Who is home, and when, is what decides whether this breed suits you. It is our first question.</p>
  </section>
</PageShell>
